                {
                  "type": "quiz",
                  "question": "A powerlifter has prioritized only muscle hypertrophy training for two years. Which subsystem is most at risk of becoming the limiting factor in his performance and longevity?",
                  "options": [
                    {
                      "text": "Joint health — neglected mobility and tissue work leads to ROM loss, synovial fluid stagnation, and accumulated joint dysfunction.",
                      "correct": true
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Skeletal — heavy compound lifts adequately load bones regardless of programming choices.",
                      "correct": false
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Neither — muscle hypertrophy training automatically optimizes the other subsystems.",
                      "correct": false
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Endocrine — muscle training has no impact on hormonal output.",
                      "correct": false
                    }
                  ],
                  "explanation": "Heavy compound lifts do stimulate Wolff's law (so bones still adapt), but joint mobility and tissue quality require dedicated work. Pure hypertrophy programs that skip mobility drills, full-ROM lifts, and soft tissue care lead to progressive joint dysfunction — a common limiter in older powerlifters.",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Quiz: The Neglected Subsystem</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Pure hypertrophy training has blind spots</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"190\" width=\"940\" height=\"380\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Why Joints Are the Weak Link</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"305\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hypertrophy work uses partial ranges often</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"345\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bro-split routines skip mobility drills</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"385\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Tight muscles pull joints out of alignment</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"425\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Synovial fluid needs full ROM to circulate</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"465\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Result: stiff hips, locked T-spine, sore shoulders</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"525\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Joint dysfunction caps strength progress</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"600\" width=\"940\" height=\"370\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"660\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Why Other Answers Fail</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"715\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Heavy lifts do load bones — Wolff's law applies</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"755\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hypertrophy does not auto-train mobility</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"795\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Muscle training absolutely drives endocrine output</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"855\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Older powerlifters often cite joint pain — not bone</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"885\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">issues — as the reason they retire.</text></svg>",
                    "caption": "Skipping joint work is the most common career-limiting mistake."
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "quote",
                  "title": "The Coach's Mindset",
                  "body": "\"You don't train muscles. You train movements. And movements are the integrated output of bones, joints, and muscles working as one system. Master the system, and the muscles will follow.\" Strength coach Mike Boyle's framing captures the philosophy behind this entire book. Every chapter that follows — bone anatomy and remodeling, joint mechanics and ranges of motion, muscle microstructure and fiber types, biomechanics and injury mechanisms — exists to give you a complete picture of the musculoskeletal system. When you understand how all three subsystems work together, you stop training body parts and start training athletes. You move from hoping your program works to engineering exactly the adaptations you need.",
                  "visual": {
                    "type": "diagram",
                    "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Coach's Mindset</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Train the system, not the body part</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"220\" width=\"940\" height=\"380\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"320\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#888\" font-style=\"italic\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\"You don't train muscles. You train movements.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"365\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#888\" font-style=\"italic\" text-anchor=\"middle\">And movements are the integrated output of</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"410\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#888\" font-style=\"italic\" text-anchor=\"middle\">bones, joints, and muscles working as one system.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"455\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#888\" font-style=\"italic\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Master the system, and the muscles will follow.\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"540\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">— Mike Boyle, strength coach</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"630\" width=\"940\" height=\"340\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"690\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Why This Book Exists</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"745\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The next 13 lessons unpack each subsystem in depth</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"785\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bone microstructure, remodeling, skeleton organization</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"825\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Joint types, mechanics, range of motion principles</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"865\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Muscle architecture, contraction, fiber types, hypertrophy</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"925\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Plus biomechanics, injury, and training adaptations</text></svg>",
                    "caption": "The book's purpose: integrated mastery of the system."
                  }
                }
